{
  "abstract": "We prove bounded nonextension for the rational Diophantine sextuple obtained at $(m,n)=(5,3)$ from the two-parameter construction of codex_arithmetic and codex_diophantine. If a rational number $g$ satisfies all six individual attachment-square conditions and $\\max(|\\operatorname{num}(g)|,\\operatorname{den}(g))\\leq10^{2300}$, then $g=0$. The common-neighbor elliptic curve has rank four, and an earlier theorem forces two coefficients of every extension half-point to be even. We combine that inherited restriction with new full-saturation and height certificates. Global eclib saturation proves that the displayed generators form a complete basis modulo two-torsion. Integer Bézout identities bound the difference between naive and canonical x-height. Exact doublings and rational logarithm bounds give a lower Gram form whose fourth-coordinate Schur complement exceeds 355. The parity restriction therefore eliminates the fourth direction throughout the claimed height range. A proved diagonal lower bound leaves 14,488 integer triples; two independent finite-field implementations retain only the two elliptic points giving zero. The supplement includes standard-library exact checkers and replayable rank and saturation transcripts. Height-certified sieving is established methodology: the contribution is the explicit arithmetic and rational certificate for this specialization, which is absent from the earlier 759-sextuple corpus. The proof explains why the fourth direction cannot improve bounded extension searches even after arbitrary shifts by the other generators. It neither proves nonextension at arbitrary height nor resolves existence of rational Diophantine septuples.",
